;;; Ein stdlib — `std.arith`: school arithmetic on natural numbers of any
;;; length, computed by saturation alone (M1f P1f.6; addition, S1f.6.3;
;;; subtraction and comparison, S1f.6.4; multiplication, S1f.6.5).
;;;
;;; Ein has no numbers and no arithmetic (the user's ruling of 2026-11-06).
;;; This module is a *theory* written in Ein over the ten digit names `0` to
;;; `9`: the tables of `std.digits` are the gates, and the rules below are the
;;; wiring. Every step is a table lookup whose outcome the table decides, so
;;; nothing is guessed: on a program that imports the theory and states
;;; requests, the universe has no candidate, R is {∅}, and the answer is
;;; root's fixpoint under `ein solve`, `ein saturate` and `ein test` alike.
;;; No `absent`, no `not` premise, one stratum.
;;;
;;; ── Import ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;     (import std.arith :symbols (arith))
;;;
;;; `arith` is the theory's one entry: the closing rule at the end of this
;;; file. It names every rule and closes every relation of the theory and the
;;; tables it reads, so that one name brings all of it, the rows of the tables
;;; included, and leaves nothing of the theory for the search to guess. A
;;; relation the importing program declares itself is the program's to close.
;;;
;;; ── The number ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A natural number is `z`, zero, or `(n PREFIX DIGIT)`: ten times the
;;; prefix, plus the digit. It reads in the order it is written:
;;;
;;;     7        (n z 7)
;;;     42       (n (n z 4) 2)
;;;     123078   (n (n (n (n (n (n z 1) 2) 3) 0) 7) 8)
;;;
;;; Any length; there is no width and no overflow. The **normal form** has no
;;; high zero, so zero is `z` and never `(n z 0)`. An operand may be written
;;; with high zeros and is read by value; every answer is in normal form.
;;;
;;; ── The protocol: a request fact, answered by a result fact ────────────────
;;;
;;;     (add X Y)          asked: X + Y
;;;     (sum-of X Y S)     the answer, repeating the operands as written
;;;
;;;     (sub X Y)          asked: X - Y
;;;     (diff-of X Y D R)  the answer, two numbers. D is X - Y where X is not
;;;                        the smaller, and `z` otherwise; R is what X is short
;;;                        by, Y - X, where X is the smaller, and `z` otherwise.
;;;                        So one of the two is always `z`, and both are when
;;;                        X = Y. The theory has no negative number: R is how
;;;                        it says "less than nothing, by this much"
;;;
;;;     (cmp X Y)          asked: X compared with Y
;;;     (cmp-of X Y O)     the answer: O is `lt`, `eq` or `gt`, by value, so
;;;                        5 and 005 compare `eq`
;;;
;;;     (mul X Y)          asked: X x Y
;;;     (product-of X Y P) the answer; a product has no width to overflow
;;;
;;; A program reads an answer with a goal that has a variable in the result's
;;; place, `(sum-of X Y ?s)`, and a rule of the program may match it like any
;;; fact. Nothing is computed that was not asked for: the theory enumerates no
;;; numbers.
;;;
;;; **Named constants.** `(let NAME NUMBER)` names a number, and a request may
;;; name an operand: with `(let price …)` and `(let qty …)`, `(add price qty)`
;;; is answered by `(sum-of price qty S)`, the names repeated as written. The
;;; same sum written with the numbers is another request, with its own answer.
;;; A name stands for a number, not for another name, and a name no `let`
;;; states is an operand that is never answered.
;;;
;;; ── How it computes (design K of P1f.6's `design.md`, round 3) ─────────────
;;;
;;;   1. **The operands.** Each is looked up: a number stands for itself, a
;;;      name for the number its `let` states.
;;;   2. **One walk from the units,** which are outermost: each column goes
;;;      through the full adder's table with the carry, and its digit is put
;;;      *in front of* an accumulator, `(b DIGIT REST)`. So the accumulator
;;;      holds the answer most significant digit outermost: a second shape,
;;;      which only the theory writes.
;;;   3. **High zeros are dropped there,** where they are outermost.
;;;   4. **The answer is turned round** into the public shape, a firing per
;;;      digit, and handed over as `(done TAG ANSWER)`.
;;;
;;; **Every call carries its continuation** as a TAG, a nested fact that holds
;;; what the caller needs next: `(sum X Y)` for a public sum. The rule that
;;; goes on has one premise, `(done (sum ?x ?y) ?s)`, and reads everything out
;;; of the tag.
;;;
;;; **Subtraction is two walks,** X - Y and Y - X, each over the full
;;; subtractor's table. The one that ends with no borrow hands its answer
;;; over; the one that ends with a borrow says `(short TAG)` and no number.
;;; Which of the two was the smaller is not known before the top column, and
;;; nothing is guessed: both are computed. **Comparison is one walk** over the
;;; comparator's table and builds no number; it hands over `(order-is TAG O)`.
;;;
;;; **Multiplication is the school method, from the multiplier's top digit.**
;;; The multiplier is turned round once, so that its top digit comes first.
;;; For each digit, the multiplicand times that digit is a row, one walk over
;;; the digit multiplier's table; the sum so far times ten, which is one
;;; constructor, `(n SUM 0)`, plus the row, is the addition walk above. The
;;; tag of each call holds the multiplier's digits still to come.
;;;
;;; ── THE RULE OF THE THEORY: no rule joins two facts on a number ────────────
;;;
;;; A rule here has **one** premise that holds numbers. Its other premises are
;;; rows of `std.digits`, looked up by digits. The reason is measured: the
;;; engine's fact index does not key on a nested fact (E-3 of P1f.6's
;;; `findings.md`), so a join of two facts on a number scans a relation for
;;; every fact of the other, and a batch of requests would cost the square of
;;; its count. Written this way, a batch costs in proportion to its count.
;;; `ein-infer/tests/arith_theory.rs` holds the rule on this file's text, and
;;; `arith_batch.rs` holds the proportion on a counter of the matcher's work.
;;; Whoever adds a rule keeps to it: a second premise that looks a fact up by
;;; a number, with a slot of its own left to bind, changes no answer and
;;; makes every batch quadratic.
;;;
;;; The one stated exception is `operand-name`, which joins an operand with a
;;; `(let NAME NUMBER)` **on the name**, and its premises are in the order
;;; that keeps that true. It first asks `(named ?x)`, a fact with every
;;; argument bound, which the engine answers by one lookup whatever `?x` is;
;;; only a name gets past it to the `let`, which the index finds by the name.
;;; So an operand that is a number costs one lookup however many `let`s the
;;; program states. Written without `(named ?x)`, every number operand walked
;;; every `let` (the stage's record has both measurements).
;;;
;;; ── The names this module occupies ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Imported flat, these names mean what this file says, and a program must
;;; not use them otherwise: `z`, `n`, `b`, `let`; the requests `add`, `sub`,
;;; `cmp` and `mul`; the results `sum-of`, `diff-of`, `cmp-of` and
;;; `product-of`, and the orders `lt`, `eq` and `gt`; the circuit's own
;;; `named`, `operand`, `operand-is`, `add-walk`, `sub-walk`, `cmp-walk`,
;;; `mul-walk`, `strip`, `turn`, `top-first`, `top-first-is`, `done`, `short`,
;;; `order-is`, `mul-call`, `mul-step`; the tags `sum`, `add-first`,
;;; `add-second`, `difference`, `shortfall`, `sub-first`, `sub-second`,
;;; `order`, `cmp-first`, `cmp-second`, `product`, `mul-first`, `mul-second`,
;;; `mul-digits`, `mul-row-of`, `mul-sum`; `arith-rule`; and the tables of
;;; `std.digits` it reads, `fa`, `fs`, `md`, `cl` and `nz`. The interface is
;;; `n`, `z`, `let`, the requests, the results and the three orders; the rest
;;; is in the state for a query or a `--trace` to see, and is not a promise.

(import std.digits :symbols (fa fs md cl nz))

;; ── The number, and the accumulator ─────────────────────────────────────────
(relation n Number Digit
  :why "the number written {?1}, then the digit {?2}")
(relation b Digit Digits
  :why "the digit {?1} in front of the digits {?2}")

;; ── The protocol ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(relation let Name Number
  :why "{?1} names the number {?2}")
(relation add Operand Operand
  :why "asked: {?1} + {?2}")
(relation sum-of Operand Operand Number
  :why "{?1} + {?2} = {?3}")
(relation sub Operand Operand
  :why "asked: {?1} - {?2}")
(relation diff-of Operand Operand Number Number
  :why "{?1} - {?2} = {?3}, short by {?4}")
(relation cmp Operand Operand
  :why "asked: {?1} compared with {?2}")
(relation cmp-of Operand Operand Order
  :why "{?1} against {?2}: {?3}")
(relation mul Operand Operand
  :why "asked: {?1} x {?2}")
(relation product-of Operand Operand Number
  :why "{?1} x {?2} = {?3}")

;; ── The circuit's own facts: each is one step of a computation, and carries
;;    the whole of its state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
(relation named Name
  :why "{?1} is a named constant")
(relation operand Tag Operand
  :why "wanted, for {?1}: the number {?2} stands for")
(relation operand-is Tag Number
  :why "found, for {?1}: the number {?2}")
(relation add-walk Tag Number Number Digit Digits
  :why "adding, for {?1}: {?2} and {?3} are still to add, the carry is {?4}, and the digits so far are {?5}")
(relation strip Tag Digits
  :why "for {?1}: the digits {?2}, high zeros not yet dropped")
(relation turn Tag Digits Number
  :why "for {?1}: the digits {?2} are still to turn round, and {?3} is turned")
(relation done Tag Number
  :why "the answer for {?1} is {?2}")
(relation sub-walk Tag Number Number Digit Digits
  :why "subtracting, for {?1}: {?3} is still to take from {?2}, the borrow is {?4}, and the digits so far are {?5}")
(relation short Tag
  :why "{?1} has no answer: the number taken from is the smaller")
(relation cmp-walk Tag Number Number Order
  :why "comparing, for {?1}: {?2} and {?3} are still to compare, and the order of the digits below is {?4}")
(relation order-is Tag Order
  :why "the answer for {?1} is {?2}")
(relation top-first Tag Number Digits
  :why "for {?1}: {?2} is still to turn round, and the digits turned are {?3}")
(relation top-first-is Tag Digits
  :why "for {?1}: the digits, the top one first, are {?2}")
(relation mul-walk Tag Number Digit Digit Digits
  :why "multiplying, for {?1}: {?2} is still to multiply by {?3}, the carry is {?4}, and the digits so far are {?5}")
(relation mul-call Tag Number Number
  :why "wanted, for {?1}: {?2} x {?3}")
(relation mul-step Tag Number Digits Number
  :why "multiplying, for {?1}: {?2} by the digits {?3} still to come, and the sum so far is {?4}")

;; ── The tags: what a call's answer is for ───────────────────────────────────
(relation sum Operand Operand
  :why "the sum of {?1} and {?2}")
(relation add-first Operand Operand
  :why "the first operand of {?1} + {?2}")
(relation add-second Operand Operand Number
  :why "the second operand of {?1} + {?2}, whose first is {?3}")
(relation difference Operand Operand
  :why "{?1} - {?2}")
(relation shortfall Operand Operand
  :why "what {?1} is short of {?2} by")
(relation sub-first Operand Operand
  :why "the first operand of {?1} - {?2}")
(relation sub-second Operand Operand Number
  :why "the second operand of {?1} - {?2}, whose first is {?3}")
(relation order Operand Operand
  :why "the order of {?1} and {?2}")
(relation cmp-first Operand Operand
  :why "the first operand of {?1} compared with {?2}")
(relation cmp-second Operand Operand Number
  :why "the second operand of {?1} compared with {?2}, whose first is {?3}")
(relation product Operand Operand
  :why "the product of {?1} and {?2}")
(relation mul-first Operand Operand
  :why "the first operand of {?1} x {?2}")
(relation mul-second Operand Operand Number
  :why "the second operand of {?1} x {?2}, whose first is {?3}")
(relation mul-digits Tag Number
  :why "the digits of the multiplier, for {?1}, which multiplies {?2}")
(relation mul-row-of Tag Number Digits Number
  :why "a row, for {?1}: {?2} times one digit, with the digits {?3} still to come and the sum so far {?4}")
(relation mul-sum Tag Number Digits
  :why "the sum so far, for {?1}, which multiplies {?2}, with the digits {?3} still to come")

;; ── The operands ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; A number stands for itself, whatever high zeros it is written with; the
;; walk reads it by value. Two rules, because "is a number" has two shapes
;; and no negation is used to tell a number from a name.
(rule operand-zero ()
  :match  (operand ?k z)
  :assert (operand-is ?k z)
  :why    "zero stands for itself")

(rule operand-number ()
  :match  (operand ?k (n ?p ?a))
  :assert (operand-is ?k (n ?p ?a))
  :why    "a number stands for itself")

;; The stated exception to the rule of the theory (the header): a join on the
;; NAME. `(named ?x)` comes first and must: every argument of it is bound, so
;; it is one lookup, and an operand that is a number stops there and never
;; walks the `let` facts. `arith_theory.rs` holds the order.
(rule let-named ()
  :match  (let ?x ?v)
  :assert (named ?x)
  :why    "{?x} is the name of a number")

(rule operand-name ()
  :match  (and (operand ?k ?x) (named ?x) (let ?x ?v))
  :assert (operand-is ?k ?v)
  :why    "{?x} names {?v}")

;; ── The walk shared by every circuit that adds ──────────────────────────────
;; `(add-walk TAG X Y CARRY ACC)`: one column a firing, from the units. A
;; number that has run out counts as zeros, so the two operands need not be of
;; one length.
(rule add-column ()
  :match  (and (add-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) (n ?q ?c) ?e ?acc)
               (fa ?a ?c ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (add-walk ?k ?p ?q ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "{?a} + {?c} + carry {?e} is {?s}, carry {?f}: {?s} goes in front of the digits so far")

(rule add-column-first ()
  :match  (and (add-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) z ?e ?acc)
               (fa ?a 0 ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (add-walk ?k ?p z ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "the second number has run out: {?a} + carry {?e} is {?s}, carry {?f}")

(rule add-column-second ()
  :match  (and (add-walk ?k z (n ?q ?c) ?e ?acc)
               (fa 0 ?c ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (add-walk ?k z ?q ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "the first number has run out: {?c} + carry {?e} is {?s}, carry {?f}")

(rule add-end ()
  :match  (add-walk ?k z z 0 ?acc)
  :assert (strip ?k ?acc)
  :why    "no column is left and no carry: the digits are {?acc}")

;; A carry out of the top column is the top digit, and it is not zero, so the
;; digits go straight to the turn.
(rule add-carry-out ()
  :match  (add-walk ?k z z 1 ?acc)
  :assert (turn ?k (b 1 ?acc) z)
  :why    "no column is left: the carry is the top digit, in front of {?acc}")

;; ── The normal form: high zeros dropped, then the turn ──────────────────────
;; The accumulator has its most significant digit outermost, so a high zero is
;; the first thing a rule sees.
(rule strip-zero ()
  :match  (strip ?k (b 0 ?r))
  :assert (strip ?k ?r)
  :why    "a high zero is dropped")

(rule strip-top ()
  :match  (and (strip ?k (b ?a ?r)) (nz ?a))
  :assert (turn ?k (b ?a ?r) z)
  :why    "the top digit {?a} is not zero: no high zero is left")

(rule strip-none ()
  :match  (strip ?k z)
  :assert (done ?k z)
  :why    "no digit is left: the answer is zero")

(rule turn-step ()
  :match  (turn ?k (b ?a ?r) ?acc)
  :assert (turn ?k ?r (n ?acc ?a))
  :why    "the digit {?a} goes after {?acc}")

(rule turn-end ()
  :match  (turn ?k z ?acc)
  :assert (done ?k ?acc)
  :why    "turned round: the answer is {?acc}")

;; ── Addition ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(rule add-ask ()
  :match  (add ?x ?y)
  :assert (operand (add-first ?x ?y) ?x)
  :why    "{?x} + {?y} is asked: first, the number {?x} stands for")

(rule add-ask-second ()
  :match  (operand-is (add-first ?x ?y) ?u)
  :assert (operand (add-second ?x ?y ?u) ?y)
  :why    "the first operand is {?u}: now the number {?y} stands for")

(rule add-start ()
  :match  (operand-is (add-second ?x ?y ?u) ?v)
  :assert (add-walk (sum ?x ?y) ?u ?v 0 z)
  :why    "add {?u} and {?v} from the units, with no carry in")

(rule add-answer ()
  :match  (done (sum ?x ?y) ?s)
  :assert (sum-of ?x ?y ?s)
  :why    "{?x} + {?y} = {?s}")

;; ── Subtraction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; `(sub-walk TAG X Y BORROW ACC)`: X - Y, one column a firing, from the
;; units, as `add-walk` goes. A walk that ends with a borrow has no answer.
(rule sub-column ()
  :match  (and (sub-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) (n ?q ?c) ?e ?acc)
               (fs ?a ?c ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (sub-walk ?k ?p ?q ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "{?a} - {?c} - borrow {?e} is {?s}, borrow {?f}: {?s} goes in front of the digits so far")

(rule sub-column-first ()
  :match  (and (sub-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) z ?e ?acc)
               (fs ?a 0 ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (sub-walk ?k ?p z ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "the number taken away has run out: {?a} - borrow {?e} is {?s}, borrow {?f}")

(rule sub-column-second ()
  :match  (and (sub-walk ?k z (n ?q ?c) ?e ?acc)
               (fs 0 ?c ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (sub-walk ?k z ?q ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "the number taken from has run out: 0 - {?c} - borrow {?e} is {?s}, borrow {?f}")

(rule sub-end ()
  :match  (sub-walk ?k z z 0 ?acc)
  :assert (strip ?k ?acc)
  :why    "no column is left and no borrow: the digits are {?acc}")

(rule sub-borrow-out ()
  :match  (sub-walk ?k z z 1 ?acc)
  :assert (short ?k)
  :why    "no column is left and a borrow is: the number taken from is the smaller")

;; Both ways at once: which number is the smaller is known at the top column
;; and not before, and the theory does not guess.
(rule sub-ask ()
  :match  (sub ?x ?y)
  :assert (operand (sub-first ?x ?y) ?x)
  :why    "{?x} - {?y} is asked: first, the number {?x} stands for")

(rule sub-ask-second ()
  :match  (operand-is (sub-first ?x ?y) ?u)
  :assert (operand (sub-second ?x ?y ?u) ?y)
  :why    "the first operand is {?u}: now the number {?y} stands for")

(rule sub-start ()
  :match  (operand-is (sub-second ?x ?y ?u) ?v)
  :assert (and (sub-walk (difference ?x ?y) ?u ?v 0 z)
               (sub-walk (shortfall ?x ?y) ?v ?u 0 z))
  :why    "take {?v} from {?u}, and {?u} from {?v}, from the units, with no borrow in: one of the two has an answer")

(rule sub-answer ()
  :match  (done (difference ?x ?y) ?d)
  :assert (diff-of ?x ?y ?d z)
  :why    "{?x} - {?y} = {?d}, and nothing is short")

(rule sub-answer-short ()
  :match  (done (shortfall ?x ?y) ?r)
  :assert (diff-of ?x ?y z ?r)
  :why    "{?x} is short of {?y} by {?r}: the difference is zero")

;; ── Comparison ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; `(cmp-walk TAG X Y ORDER)`: one column a firing, from the units; ORDER is
;; the order of the digits already passed, `eq` before the first. A higher
;; digit overrules it, which is the comparator's table. A number that has run
;; out counts as zeros, so the order is by value.
(rule cmp-column ()
  :match  (and (cmp-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) (n ?q ?c) ?o)
               (cl ?o ?a ?c ?t))
  :assert (cmp-walk ?k ?p ?q ?t)
  :why    "the order below is {?o}; {?a} against {?c} above it makes it {?t}")

(rule cmp-column-first ()
  :match  (and (cmp-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) z ?o)
               (cl ?o ?a 0 ?t))
  :assert (cmp-walk ?k ?p z ?t)
  :why    "the second number has run out: the order below is {?o}, and {?a} against nothing makes it {?t}")

(rule cmp-column-second ()
  :match  (and (cmp-walk ?k z (n ?q ?c) ?o)
               (cl ?o 0 ?c ?t))
  :assert (cmp-walk ?k z ?q ?t)
  :why    "the first number has run out: the order below is {?o}, and nothing against {?c} makes it {?t}")

(rule cmp-end ()
  :match  (cmp-walk ?k z z ?o)
  :assert (order-is ?k ?o)
  :why    "no column is left: the order is {?o}")

(rule cmp-ask ()
  :match  (cmp ?x ?y)
  :assert (operand (cmp-first ?x ?y) ?x)
  :why    "{?x} compared with {?y} is asked: first, the number {?x} stands for")

(rule cmp-ask-second ()
  :match  (operand-is (cmp-first ?x ?y) ?u)
  :assert (operand (cmp-second ?x ?y ?u) ?y)
  :why    "the first operand is {?u}: now the number {?y} stands for")

(rule cmp-start ()
  :match  (operand-is (cmp-second ?x ?y ?u) ?v)
  :assert (cmp-walk (order ?x ?y) ?u ?v eq)
  :why    "compare {?u} with {?v} from the units: equal so far")

(rule cmp-answer ()
  :match  (order-is (order ?x ?y) ?o)
  :assert (cmp-of ?x ?y ?o)
  :why    "{?x} against {?y}: {?o}")

;; ── Multiplication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; The school method from the multiplier's top digit: the sum so far times
;; ten, plus the multiplicand times the next digit.

;; `(top-first TAG NUMBER DIGITS)`: a number turned round, so that its most
;; significant digit is outermost: the other turn. It hands its digits over
;; as `(top-first-is TAG DIGITS)`.
(rule top-first-step ()
  :match  (top-first ?k (n ?p ?a) ?acc)
  :assert (top-first ?k ?p (b ?a ?acc))
  :why    "the digit {?a} goes in front of {?acc}")

(rule top-first-end ()
  :match  (top-first ?k z ?acc)
  :assert (top-first-is ?k ?acc)
  :why    "turned round: the digits, the top one first, are {?acc}")

;; `(mul-walk TAG X DIGIT CARRY ACC)`: X times one digit, a column a firing
;; from the units, through the digit multiplier's table.
(rule mul-column ()
  :match  (and (mul-walk ?k (n ?p ?a) ?m ?e ?acc)
               (md ?a ?m ?e ?s ?f))
  :assert (mul-walk ?k ?p ?m ?f (b ?s ?acc))
  :why    "{?a} x {?m} + carry {?e} is {?s}, carry {?f}: {?s} goes in front of the digits so far")

(rule mul-walk-end ()
  :match  (mul-walk ?k z ?m 0 ?acc)
  :assert (strip ?k ?acc)
  :why    "no column is left and no carry: the digits are {?acc}")

;; A carry out of the top column is the top digit, and it is not zero.
(rule mul-carry-out ()
  :match  (and (mul-walk ?k z ?m ?e ?acc) (nz ?e))
  :assert (turn ?k (b ?e ?acc) z)
  :why    "no column is left: the carry {?e} is the top digit, in front of {?acc}")

;; `(mul-call TAG X Y)`: X times Y for whoever TAG says, answered by
;; `(done TAG PRODUCT)`. `(mul-step TAG X DIGITS SUM)`: the multiplier's
;; digits still to come, the top one first, and the sum so far.
(rule mul-turn ()
  :match  (mul-call ?k ?u ?v)
  :assert (top-first (mul-digits ?k ?u) ?v z)
  :why    "to multiply {?u} by {?v}: first {?v}'s digits, the top one first")

(rule mul-begin ()
  :match  (top-first-is (mul-digits ?k ?u) ?t)
  :assert (mul-step ?k ?u ?t z)
  :why    "nothing is summed yet")

(rule mul-row ()
  :match  (mul-step ?k ?u (b ?a ?rest) ?acc)
  :assert (mul-walk (mul-row-of ?k ?u ?rest ?acc) ?u ?a 0 z)
  :why    "the next digit of the multiplier is {?a}: the row {?u} x {?a}")

(rule mul-first-row ()
  :match  (done (mul-row-of ?k ?u ?rest z) ?row)
  :assert (mul-step ?k ?u ?rest ?row)
  :why    "nothing was summed yet: the row {?row} is the sum so far")

(rule mul-add-row ()
  :match  (done (mul-row-of ?k ?u ?rest (n ?p ?a)) ?row)
  :assert (add-walk (mul-sum ?k ?u ?rest) (n (n ?p ?a) 0) ?row 0 z)
  :why    "ten times the sum so far, plus the row {?row}")

(rule mul-next ()
  :match  (done (mul-sum ?k ?u ?rest) ?s)
  :assert (mul-step ?k ?u ?rest ?s)
  :why    "the sum so far is {?s}")

(rule mul-end ()
  :match  (mul-step ?k ?u z ?acc)
  :assert (done ?k ?acc)
  :why    "no digit of the multiplier is left: the product is {?acc}")

(rule mul-ask ()
  :match  (mul ?x ?y)
  :assert (operand (mul-first ?x ?y) ?x)
  :why    "{?x} x {?y} is asked: first, the number {?x} stands for")

(rule mul-ask-second ()
  :match  (operand-is (mul-first ?x ?y) ?u)
  :assert (operand (mul-second ?x ?y ?u) ?y)
  :why    "the first operand is {?u}: now the number {?y} stands for")

(rule mul-start ()
  :match  (operand-is (mul-second ?x ?y ?u) ?v)
  :assert (mul-call (product ?x ?y) ?u ?v)
  :why    "multiply {?u} by {?v}")

(rule mul-answer ()
  :match  (done (product ?x ?y) ?p)
  :assert (product-of ?x ?y ?p)
  :why    "{?x} x {?y} = {?p}")

;; ── The closing rule, and the theory's one entry ────────────────────────────
;; Arithmetic is computed and never guessed: every relation of the theory, and
;; each table it reads, is closed, so the search has no candidate among them.
;; The rule also names every rule of the module, which is what makes
;; `(import std.arith :symbols (arith))` bring the whole theory: a selection's
;; closure follows the names a kept declaration writes.
;; `ein-infer/tests/arith_theory.rs` holds that no relation and no rule of
;; this file is missing here.
(relation arith-rule Rule
  :why "{?1} is a rule of std.arith")

;; The closing rule's own name, as a written fact. A selection brings it,
;; since `arith-rule` is a name `arith` writes, and it is what keeps `arith`
;; in a program that is inlined and tree-shaken, as the program a
;; `--dump-answer` state file re-writes is: the tree-shake keeps a rule that a
;; name or a premise reaches, and nothing else reaches a rule that matches on
;; nothing (E-11 of P1f.6's `findings.md`).
(arith-rule arith)

(rule arith ()
  :match  ()
  :assert (and (__closed__ fa) (__closed__ fs) (__closed__ md) (__closed__ cl)
               (__closed__ nz)
               (__closed__ n) (__closed__ b)
               (__closed__ let) (__closed__ add) (__closed__ sum-of)
               (__closed__ sub) (__closed__ diff-of)
               (__closed__ cmp) (__closed__ cmp-of)
               (__closed__ mul) (__closed__ product-of)
               (__closed__ named) (__closed__ operand) (__closed__ operand-is)
               (__closed__ add-walk) (__closed__ strip) (__closed__ turn)
               (__closed__ done)
               (__closed__ sub-walk) (__closed__ short)
               (__closed__ cmp-walk) (__closed__ order-is)
               (__closed__ top-first) (__closed__ top-first-is)
               (__closed__ mul-walk) (__closed__ mul-call) (__closed__ mul-step)
               (__closed__ sum) (__closed__ add-first) (__closed__ add-second)
               (__closed__ difference) (__closed__ shortfall)
               (__closed__ sub-first) (__closed__ sub-second)
               (__closed__ order) (__closed__ cmp-first) (__closed__ cmp-second)
               (__closed__ product) (__closed__ mul-first) (__closed__ mul-second)
               (__closed__ mul-digits) (__closed__ mul-row-of) (__closed__ mul-sum)
               (__closed__ arith-rule)
               (arith-rule operand-zero) (arith-rule operand-number)
               (arith-rule let-named) (arith-rule operand-name)
               (arith-rule add-column) (arith-rule add-column-first)
               (arith-rule add-column-second)
               (arith-rule add-end) (arith-rule add-carry-out)
               (arith-rule strip-zero) (arith-rule strip-top)
               (arith-rule strip-none)
               (arith-rule turn-step) (arith-rule turn-end)
               (arith-rule add-ask) (arith-rule add-ask-second)
               (arith-rule add-start) (arith-rule add-answer)
               (arith-rule sub-column) (arith-rule sub-column-first)
               (arith-rule sub-column-second)
               (arith-rule sub-end) (arith-rule sub-borrow-out)
               (arith-rule sub-ask) (arith-rule sub-ask-second)
               (arith-rule sub-start)
               (arith-rule sub-answer) (arith-rule sub-answer-short)
               (arith-rule cmp-column) (arith-rule cmp-column-first)
               (arith-rule cmp-column-second) (arith-rule cmp-end)
               (arith-rule cmp-ask) (arith-rule cmp-ask-second)
               (arith-rule cmp-start) (arith-rule cmp-answer)
               (arith-rule top-first-step) (arith-rule top-first-end)
               (arith-rule mul-column) (arith-rule mul-walk-end)
               (arith-rule mul-carry-out)
               (arith-rule mul-turn) (arith-rule mul-begin)
               (arith-rule mul-row) (arith-rule mul-first-row)
               (arith-rule mul-add-row) (arith-rule mul-next)
               (arith-rule mul-end)
               (arith-rule mul-ask) (arith-rule mul-ask-second)
               (arith-rule mul-start) (arith-rule mul-answer))
  :why    "arithmetic is computed and never guessed: every relation of the theory is closed")
